Key Business Findings
1. Booking Value Across Operational Variables
Business Question
Which operational factors significantly influence booking value?
Statistical Results
Kruskal–Wallis testing evaluated booking value across:
•	Booking Status 
•	Vehicle Type 
•	Pickup Location 
•	Drop Location 
•	Payment Method 
•	Ride Status 
•	Outcome Reason 
Results:
Variable	H Statistic	p-value	Conclusion
Booking Status	0.896	0.826	Not Significant
Vehicle Type	6.792	0.341	Not Significant
Pickup Location	49.180	0.466	Not Significant
Drop Location	55.867	0.233	Not Significant
Payment Method	4.921	0.178	Not Significant
Ride Status	8.444	0.0147	Significant
Outcome Reason	18.842	0.064	Not Significant
Business Interpretation
Among all operational variables tested, Ride Status was the only factor that significantly influenced booking value, indicating that completed, cancelled, and incomplete rides generate meaningfully different revenue outcomes.
________________________________________
2. Ride Status Performance
Statistical Results
Booking Value Distribution:
Ride Status	Booking Value	Share
Completed	₹38.33 Million	67.80%
Cancelled	₹15.95 Million	28.22%
Incomplete	₹2.25 Million	3.98%
Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit:
•	χ² = 6348.26 
•	p < 0.001 
Business Interpretation
Completed rides account for over two-thirds of total booking revenue, while cancelled and incomplete rides collectively represent 32.2% of potential booking value, highlighting a significant opportunity for revenue recovery.
Business Recommendation
Reducing cancellations and incomplete rides should be a primary operational KPI due to their direct impact on realized revenue.
________________________________________
3. Day of Week Analysis
Business Question
Does booking value vary by day of the week?
Statistical Results
•	H = 6.761 
•	p = 0.344 
Business Interpretation
Booking values remain statistically consistent throughout the week.
Business Recommendation
Dynamic pricing and operational planning should rely on demand forecasting rather than weekday-specific booking values.
________________________________________
4. Ride Distance Analysis
Business Question
Do longer rides generate significantly higher booking values?
Statistical Results
Distance Bracket Analysis:
•	H = 0.900 
•	p = 0.925 
Business Interpretation
Despite higher total revenue from certain distance brackets, booking values do not differ significantly across ride distance categories.
Business Recommendation
Revenue optimization should focus on increasing completed ride volume rather than targeting specific trip distances.
________________________________________
5. Customer vs Driver Ratings
Business Question
Do highly rated drivers receive higher customer ratings?
Statistical Results
Pearson Correlation:
•	r = -0.00281 
Spearman Correlation:
•	ρ = -0.00280 
Business Interpretation
Virtually no correlation exists between driver ratings and customer ratings.
Business Recommendation
Driver performance should be evaluated using multiple operational KPIs rather than ratings alone.
________________________________________
6. Operational Associations
Business Question
Which operational variables are associated with one another?
Chi-Square Results
Most variable pairs demonstrated very weak associations.
Examples:
Variable Pair	χ²	Cramer's V	Association
Booking Status ↔ Pickup Location	170.44	0.023	No Meaningful Association
Pickup ↔ Drop Location	2466.38	0.022	Weak
Booking Status ↔ Vehicle Type	Weak	Low Effect Size	No
Booking Status ↔ Payment Method	Weak	Low Effect Size	No
Business Interpretation
Operational variables are largely independent, indicating that cancellations and booking outcomes are unlikely to be explained by location, payment method, or vehicle type alone.
________________________________________
Overall Business Impact
Using 20+ statistical hypothesis tests across 103,024 ride bookings, this project transformed operational ride data into statistically validated business insights.
The analysis:
•	Evaluated 7+ operational variables affecting booking performance. 
•	Identified Ride Status as the only statistically significant driver of booking value (H = 8.444, p = 0.0147). 
•	Quantified that Completed rides generated ₹38.33 million (67.8%) of the ₹56.54 million total booking value, while cancelled and incomplete rides represented 32.2% of unrealized revenue. 
•	Confirmed that ride distance, payment method, vehicle type, booking status, pickup location, and day of week did not significantly influence booking value. 
•	Demonstrated near-zero correlation between customer and driver ratings (r ≈ -0.003), indicating ratings alone are insufficient for performance evaluation. 
•	Found no meaningful operational associations among most categorical variables, suggesting improvements should focus on ride execution and cancellation reduction rather than customer segmentation. 
________________________________________
Business Recommendations
•	Prioritize reducing ride cancellations and incomplete rides, as they account for over ₹18.2 million (32.2%) of total booking value. 
•	Track Ride Completion Rate as the primary operational KPI because it is the only statistically significant determinant of booking revenue. 
•	Focus operational improvements on dispatch efficiency, driver availability, and ride execution instead of payment methods or vehicle allocation. 
•	Develop driver performance scorecards using multiple operational metrics rather than relying solely on customer ratings. 
•	Continue validating operational initiatives using statistical hypothesis testing to ensure observed improvements are genuinely significant. 
